"CONSUMER CONTRACTS\nthe U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit applying Arkansas law; and four by the U.S. Court\nof Appeals for the Ninth Circuit applying California law.\nIn the three cases in which notice of the terms was given and the hyperlinks to the terms\nwere conspicuous in ways that put a reasonably prudent user on inquiry notice of the terms, the\nbrowsewrap was enforced. MetroPCS Commc'ns, Inc. v. Porter, 273 So. 3d 1025 (Fla. Dist. Ct.\nApp. 2018); Hubbert v. Dell Corp., 835 N.E.2d 113 (Ill. App. Ct. 2005); Major v. McCalister, 302\nS.W.3d 227 (Mo. Ct. App. 2009).\nIn three cases, the court remanded to the lower court to inquire whether the notice\nrequirements had been met. Nicosia v. Amazon.com, Inc., 834 F.3d 220 (2d Cir. 2016); Foster v.\nWalmart, Inc., 15 F.4th 860 (8th Cir. 2021); Thornton v. Uber Techs., Inc., 858 S.E.2d 255 (Ga.\nCt. App. 2021). The browsewrap terms were not enforced in the remaining cases because the court\nconcluded that the notice and/or the placement and visibility of the hyperlinked terms were not\nsufficient to inform consumers about the terms. Campbell v. Gen. Dynamics Gov't Sys. Corp., 407\nF.3d 546 (1st Cir. 2005); Soliman v. Subway Franchisee Advert. Fund Tr., Ltd. 999 F.3d 828 (2d\nCir. 2021); Nicosia v. Amazon.com, Inc., 834 F.3d 220 (2d Cir. 2016); Specht v. Netscape\nCommc'ns Corp., 306 F.3d 17 (2d Cir. 2002); James v. Glob. TelLink Corp., 852 F.3d 262 (3d\nCir. 2017); Benson v. Double Down Interactive, LLC, 798 F. App'x 117 (9th Cir. 2020); Wilson\nv. Huuuge, Inc., 944 F.3d 1212 (9th Cir. 2019); Nguyen v. Barnes & Noble Inc., 763 F.3d 1171\n(9th Cir. 2014); Roller v. TV Guide Online Holdings, LLC, 2013 Ark. 285, 2013 WL 3322348\n(Ark. June 27, 2013); Long v. Provide Com., Inc., 200 Cal. Rptr. 3d 117 (Cal. Ct. App. 2016);\nVitacost.com, Inc. v. McCants, 210 So. 3d 761 (Fla. Dist. Ct. App. 2017); Hoffman v. Supplements\nTogo Mgmt., LLC, 18 A.3d 210 (N.J. Super. Ct. App. Div. 2011).\nSimilar results are obtained from a comprehensive analysis of all state and federal cases\navailable on Westlaw, Lexis, and secondary sources addressing the enforceability of browsewraps\nin consumer transactions, starting with Specht in 2002, and ending with Berman v. Freedom\nFinancial Network, LLC, 30 F.4th 849 (9th Cir. 2022). This resulted in analyzing 19 additional\ncases from state trial courts and federal district courts, both published and unpublished.\nThe examination reveals that browsewraps were enforced in eight additional cases in which\nthe website included both a prominent statement of notice and conspicuous hyperlinks to the terms.\nConversely, in the cases in which the website lacked either a prominent statement of notice or\nconspicuous hyperlinks to the terms, courts refused to enforce the browsewraps on the basis of\nfailure to provide sufficient notice.\nOf the two cases of casual browsing, when the consumer was visiting the website without\nentering into a transaction, one court held that either notice of the terms or a conspicuously placed\nhyperlink with the terms, properly labeled, constitutes sufficient notice, and thus continued use of\nthe website constitutes acceptance of the browsewrap's terms. The other court held that both notice\nof the terms and a conspicuously placed hyperlink are required. When the consumer enters into a\ntransaction with the seller, only three out of 18 cases required both factors, holding that merely\nincluding prominent hyperlinks without a statement explicitly referring to them did not constitute\nreasonable notice. (Cases in which the consumer downloads software free of charge are counted\n66\n@§2\n"